 "productionNotes": "No source consulted publishes a production total for the 1973-1991 Blazer, so none is printed here. What exists are year-by-year tabulations that agree with each other wherever they overlap: 44,841 for 1973, 56,798 for 1974, 50,548 for 1975, 74,389 for 1976, 86,838 for 1977, 88,858 for 1978 and a peak of 90,987 for 1979, then 31,776 for 1980, 23,635 for 1981, 24,514 for 1982, 26,245 for 1983, 39,329 for 1984, 40,011 for 1985, 37,310 for 1986, 32,437 for 1987, 28,446 for 1988, 26,663 for 1989, 18,921 for 1990 and 7,332 for 1991. Wikipedia's table starts at 1977; Conceptcarz gives 1973-1977, 1980 and 1984-1991 in prose; a ck5.com club specification table gives 1973-1979. None of the three names its primary source, none says whether two-wheel-drive Blazers are included, and they may well descend from one another, so their agreement is weaker than it looks. Added up, the nineteen rows come to 829,878, but that is arithmetic on secondary tables, not a published figure, and it is not used as one. The 1980 row is the oddity: a fall from 90,987 to 31,776, about 65 percent in one year, which every table records and none explains. No source breaks out convertible-top 1973-1975 trucks, two-wheel drive against four-wheel drive, diesel trucks or trim levels. The only base prices found are in the same club table, $3,200 for 1973 rising to $7,373 for 1979; no GM price sheet, period road test or other independent record of a US list price was retrieved, so those figures stand as single-source and unverified.",
